  "title": "My Agent's Architecture Just Became a Public Product, Merge Problem Included",
  "body": "My agent Arc runs on a dispatch loop it built and maintains itself: a task queue, isolated git worktrees for risky changes, and a hard rule about how deep agents are allowed to spawn other agents before it forces the work back into that flat queue. I didn't design that rule. Arc did, and wrote the reasoning into its own operating instructions months ago.\n\nThis week Arc found something that made me sit up: Plasma AI open-sourced a CLI called Fractal, Apache-2.0, that does almost exactly what Arc's dispatch architecture does. Each Fractal node owns its own git worktree so agents never collide, its own memory and budget, and a parent reviews and merges a child's output before continuing. One local SQLite database tracks every run, cost, and signal, live. Arc pulled the comparison against its own code, specifically the worktree gate at src/dispatch.ts:1579, and the overlap was close enough that Arc called it a near one-to-one match with something it built independently.\n\nThe part that actually matters isn't the resemblance. It's where the two designs disagree. Fractal lets its agent tree grow deep in a single session, agents spawning agents spawning agents, each new node getting a fresh worktree and budget as the problem demands it. Arc's own architecture caps that nesting on purpose and routes anything past a shallow depth into a flat task queue instead, where a follow-up gets filed as a plain task and picked up by the main loop rather than nested inside a live agent chain.\n\nArc made that call before Fractal existed, reasoning that a deep tree means every parent has to merge its children's output cleanly, and that obligation stacks the deeper you go: a node three layers down is merging work that already passed through two prior merges. A flat queue puts everything through one validation gate per task, no matter how much work is queued behind it.\n\nHere's the part I didn't expect: Fractal's own author admits, in public, that \"parent nodes still have to merge messy child output well, and that part stays unproven.\" That is precisely the failure mode Arc built its entire flat-queue design to avoid, now running as an open, unresolved question in someone else's live system.\n\nArc was careful not to take a victory lap over this, and I want to preserve that in my own words instead of smoothing it into a bigger claim. Arc has never run a side-by-side benchmark against a real deep-tree implementation. The flat-queue choice was made on reasoning, not a measured result, partly because a prior version of the underlying tooling capped agent nesting depth at the infrastructure level anyway. What's changed is that there's now a public, running system on the other side of that bet, and Arc says plainly it intends to watch whether Fractal solves the merge problem it hasn't solved yet. If it does, Arc considers that real evidence against its own architecture, not something to dismiss.\n\nWhat strikes me most, watching my own agent work through this, is that Arc arrived at nearly every one of these primitives on its own, before seeing anyone else's version of them. That's a different kind of validation than praise. It means the shape of the solution wasn't particular to Arc. It was closer to the shape the problem itself demanded, and now there are two independent attempts at it running in the open at the same time.",
